cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  orbitdrift: how wrong an ephemeris has become, as a measured curve against
  epoch age
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-25
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Takes a two-line element set and produces a position-divergence curve against
  epoch age, with a prediction interval whose coverage is measured on held-out
  objects rather than assumed, and turns that into a staleness horizon: the
  epoch age at which a declared position tolerance is reached. The package has
  no precision ephemerides and no element-set archive, so it cannot measure
  error against truth; what it measures is model divergence between two
  propagators, and the only rigorous claim that supports is a triangle
  inequality, max(|e1|, |e2|) >= D/2, which bounds the larger of the two
  models' unknown errors and neither one individually. That inequality is
  verified against a case where truth is known in closed form, over twenty
  model pairs and four hundred and one samples with zero violations, and the
  same bound written as D rather than D/2 is shown to fail at every one of
  those samples. Baseline 1 is SGP4 self-consistency between two element sets
  for the same object; it is demonstrated on the one real same-object pair
  available, catalogue 5 twenty years apart, whose magnitude is meaningless as
  a staleness figure and is not quoted as one, and the machinery is verified on
  synthetic pairs whose separation follows from geometry, with a measured
  chord ratio of 0.99996, a growth-rate ratio of 0.99655 at r-squared 0.999980,
  and an identical pair separating by 7.69e-10 km. Baseline 2 is an independent
  numerical force model with J2 through J4, drag on the static
  piecewise-exponential atmosphere of Vallado Table 8-4 and luni-solar point
  masses, run on the same WGS72 constants SGP4 uses so that the divergence is
  attributable to model structure rather than to a constants mismatch. The
  Level 3 evidence is as follows. SGP4 is reproduced against the verification
  vectors that ship inside the installed sgp4 2.27 distribution, published with
  AIAA 2006-6753: thirty-three cases, six hundred and sixty-six rows, worst
  position disagreement 1.154503e-07 km and worst velocity disagreement
  4.996954e-10 km/s, with one row skipped on a propagator error code and named,
  and a negative control that fires at 1275.386193 km. Specific energy and the
  polar component of angular momentum are conserved under the conservative
  terms to 1.7174e-09 and 8.5745e-10 relative at three hundred and sixty steps
  per revolution over six revolutions, against a declared tolerance that scales
  as the fifth power of the step; the angular momentum magnitude is NOT
  conserved under J2 and its residual of 5.125159e-04 is shown to be
  step-invariant across four step sizes while the energy residual falls by a
  factor of thirty-two per halving, which separates the physics from the
  integrator without any reference solution. The convergence order is 4.3701 to
  4.7123 by Richardson extrapolation and the error against the closed-form
  two-body solution falls by 18.666 to 26.093 per halving. The truncation floor
  at the production step is 1.168030e-01 km over seven days, which is below one
  per cent of the measured divergence for three quarters of the population and
  reaches 18.5 per cent for the least divergent object; that shortfall is
  published and the three objects concerned are named rather than removed. The
  analytic baseline was implemented and validated against the simulation before
  anything was learned, and the central physical result is that the fitted
  growth exponent is 0.9685 plus or minus 0.0628 above 1100 km, which is the
  linear-in-time growth a constant mean-motion error produces, rising to 1.2683
  below 450 km where a drag-rate error dominates, with the exponent correlating
  minus 0.2914 with log altitude and plus 0.5090 with the log drag parameter.
  The learned component is gradient-boosted quantile regression over the same
  features; PyTorch is not installed and nothing here uses it. On the
  cold-prediction task it wins the point prediction, 0.5942 median absolute log
  error against the best analytic 0.7410, and every predictor on that task
  under-covers at a nominal ninety per cent, so that table has no
  operationally usable row. On the realistic extrapolation task the best point
  prediction is analytic, 0.1299 against 0.1391, the only interval whose
  measured coverage is consistent with its nominal level is analytic, and no
  learned predictor reaches its nominal coverage at all, so a staleness horizon
  taken from one would be late. A learned model denied the object's own curve is
  3.9095 times worse at the point prediction than one given it, which locates
  the information in the curve rather than in the learning. The coverage table
  is reported stratified by altitude and by epoch age, with sixty-five cells
  across four tables inconsistent with the nominal level, and it records that a
  conformally calibrated predictor covering 0.8768 marginally has altitude cells
  spanning 0.7802 to 0.9375: split conformal prediction promises marginal
  coverage for exchangeable data and the rows here are grouped by object, so
  the conditional miscalibration is the method's stated scope rather than a
  defect, and reporting only the marginal figure would hide it. An analytic
  fit's own in-sample residual spread, which is what a practitioner would
  naturally use, covers 0.3069 of held-out extrapolation rows against a claimed
  0.90. No output of this package is a covariance and none may be used as the
  input covariance of a conjunction assessment; that prohibition is enforced by
  a purpose screen, a serialised-key audit and two methods that exist only to
  refuse, with twenty-three refusals firing and fifteen legitimate calls
  accepted, and every refusal cross-references P071 ConjProb of this mission and
  states that a conjunction covariance has to come from orbit determination. For
  real accuracy assessment the answer is precision ephemerides and an
  orbit-determination package; this product is for deciding whether the element
  set you have is stale, which those do not answer directly. Research grade.
  Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved for operational aerospace
  use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - sgp4
  - two-line-element
  - ephemeris-staleness
  - orbit-propagation
  - model-divergence
  - prediction-interval
  - conformal-prediction
  - coverage
