  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Clopper
        given-names: "C. J."
      - family-names: Pearson
        given-names: "E. S."
    title: >-
      The use of confidence or fiducial limits illustrated in the case of the
      binomial
    journal: "Biometrika"
    volume: 26
    issue: 4
    year: 1934
    notes: >-
      The exact binomial interval placed on every measured coverage fraction,
      including the stratified cells where it shows the coverage inconsistent
      with the nominal level.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Papadopoulos
        given-names: "H."
      - family-names: Proedrou
        given-names: "K."
      - family-names: Vovk
        given-names: "V."
      - family-names: Gammerman
        given-names: "A."
    title: "Inductive confidence machines for regression"
    collection-title: "Machine Learning: ECML 2002"
    year: 2002
    notes: >-
      Split conformal prediction, used to calibrate every interval in this
      package. Its guarantee is marginal coverage for exchangeable data; the
      rows here are grouped by object, and the resulting conditional
      miscalibration is measured and published rather than left implicit.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Lei
        given-names: "J."
      - family-names: "G'Sell"
        given-names: "M."
      - family-names: Rinaldo
        given-names: "A."
      - family-names: Tibshirani
        given-names: "R. J."
      - family-names: Wasserman
        given-names: "L."
    title: "Distribution-free predictive inference for regression"
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 113
    issue: 523
    year: 2018
    notes: >-
      The finite-sample quantile index ceil((n+1)(1-alpha))/n that makes the
      split-conformal coverage guarantee hold at finite n.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Gneiting
        given-names: "T."
      - family-names: Raftery
        given-names: "A. E."
    title: >-
      Strictly proper scoring rules, prediction, and estimation
    journal: "Journal of the American Statistical Association"
    volume: 102
    issue: 477
    year: 2007
    notes: >-
      Equation 43, the Winkler interval score, which is the single number that
      trades width against coverage and the one used to rank predictors -- but
      only among those whose measured coverage is consistent with the level they
      claim, because narrowing an interval improves its score until it stops
      covering.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Friedman
        given-names: "J. H."
    title: "Greedy function approximation: a gradient boosting machine"
    journal: "Annals of Statistics"
    volume: 29
    issue: 5
    year: 2001
    notes: >-
      The gradient-boosted quantile regressor that is the learned component. It
      wins the cold-task point prediction and loses the extrapolation-task point
      prediction to an analytic form, and no variant of it reaches its nominal
      interval coverage on the extrapolation task.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Picone
        given-names: "J. M."
      - family-names: Hedin
        given-names: "A. E."
      - family-names: Drob
        given-names: "D. P."
      - family-names: Aikin
        given-names: "A. C."
    title: >-
      NRLMSISE-00 empirical model of the atmosphere: statistical comparisons and
      scientific issues
    journal: "Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics"
    volume: 107
    issue: A12
    year: 2002
    notes: >-
      Named as the model this package does NOT use. The atmosphere here is the
      static piecewise-exponential table of Vallado 2013 Table 8-4, with no
      solar index, no diurnal bulge and no geomagnetic term, and every
      drag-driven divergence number inherits that limitation.
